#1.  Core Concepts: BackTrack vs. Kali Linux
The evolution of offensive security operating systems shifted significantly from BackTrack to Kali Linux. Both are Linux distributions specifically packaged with hundreds of pre-installed tools for penetration testing, digital forensics, and security auditing.

* BackTrack Linux (Legacy): Originally built on Slackware and later moved to Ubuntu. It used a non-standard directory hierarchy (storing penetration testing tools in /pentest/) and required manual updates or completely new OS flashes. It was officially deprecated in 2013 and is no longer updated or supported.

* Kali Linux (Modern): Rebuilt entirely from scratch on a stable Debian GNU/Linux blueprint by Offensive Security. It follows the standard Filesystem Hierarchy Standard (FHS), making it highly familiar to standard Linux users. It uses a rolling release model for continuous updates via official repositories and features a deeply customized kernel to support a vast array of wireless cards and ARM devices.

