## 3. Network Interface Card (NIC) Configuration
Penetration testing requires deep control over your Network Interface Card (NIC) to interact with targets effectively.
## Checking Your Network State

* Run ip a or ifconfig to list your active network interfaces (e.g., eth0 for wired, wlan0 for wireless, lo for loopback).

## Managing Interfaces via the Command Line

* Bring an interface down: sudo ip link set eth0 down (or sudo ifconfig eth0 down)
* Bring an interface up: sudo ip link set eth0 up (or sudo ifconfig eth0 up)

## Dynamic vs. Static IP Configuration
Kali relies on the NetworkManager service by default, but manual adjustments are completed via the /etc/network/interfaces file or the nmcli command line interface.

* To obtain an IP dynamically (DHCP):

sudo dhclient eth0

* To configure a static IP manually via command line:

sudo ip addr add 192.168.1.50/24 dev eth0
sudo ip route add default via 192.168.1.1


## Wireless NIC Modes (Crucial for Pentesting)
Wireless network cards operate in two main modes during testing:

* Managed Mode: Standard state. The card connects directly to an Access Point (AP) and only processes traffic meant for your specific machine.
* Monitor Mode: Essential for wireless auditing. The card listens to all wireless traffic passing through the air on a given channel, even if it isn't directed to your machine.
* To enable monitor mode using airmon-ng: sudo airmon-ng start wlan0

